const eraViiiOpeningNarratives = {
  'chapter-building-democratic-state-1990-1992': [
    {id:'after-democratic-breakthrough',number:'01',title:'After the Democratic Breakthrough',paragraphs:['The political opening of 1990 ended the constitutional monopoly of one party, but it did not instantly create a complete democratic state. The first multiparty election opened a transitional period in which old institutions, new political organizations, and elected representatives had to negotiate how authority would work.','This chapter begins where the 1990 movement leaves off. Its subject is institutionalization: turning political competition into rules for legislation, executive power, rights, courts, and local government.'],sourceIds:['source-mongolia-constitution-1992','source-fish-inner-asian-anomaly-2001']},
    {id:'transitional-institutions',number:'02',title:'Transitional Institutions',paragraphs:['The 1990 election produced a restructured People’s Great Hural and a standing State Baga Hural, also called the State Small Hural. These bodies connected the inherited state to the emerging multiparty order while constitutional work proceeded.','The arrangement combined continuity and change. The MPRP remained powerful, opposition representatives entered formal politics, and negotiation occurred inside institutions as well as through public political life.'],sourceIds:['source-mongolia-constitution-1992','source-fish-inner-asian-anomaly-2001']},
    {id:'writing-constitutional-order',number:'03',title:'Writing a New Constitutional Order',paragraphs:['Constitutional drafting required choices about sovereignty, representation, executive authority, courts, rights, property, and local administration. These were not technical details added after democratization; they helped define what the new political order would be.','Drafting and debate took place amid economic disruption and institutional uncertainty. Compromise therefore reflected competing political projects and the practical need to create rules under which disagreement could continue without restoring one-party monopoly.'],sourceIds:['source-mongolia-constitution-1992','source-parliament-constitution-history']},
    {id:'constitution-1992',number:'04',title:'The 1992 Constitution',paragraphs:['Mongolia adopted its new Constitution on 13 January 1992. It entered into force on 12 February 1992. Keeping these dates separate distinguishes the legislature’s act of adoption from the beginning of the Constitution’s legal operation.','The Constitution defined Mongolia as an independent republic grounded in popular sovereignty, rights, representative government, and separated state functions. It established a framework for democratic politics; it did not guarantee that institutions would always work evenly or that political development was finished.'],evidenceNote:{claim:'The Constitution was adopted on 13 January 1992 and entered into force on 12 February 1992.',sourceBasis:'Constitution of Mongolia and State Great Hural institutional history',evidenceType:'MONGOLIAN PRIMARY LAW / PARLIAMENTARY RECORD',treatment:'ESTABLISHED LEGAL CHRONOLOGY',confidence:'ESTABLISHED',caution:'Adoption and entry into force are separate legal moments; constitutional enactment is not proof of completed democratization.'},sourceIds:['source-mongolia-constitution-1992','source-parliament-constitution-history']},
    {id:'parliament-representative-government',number:'05',title:'Parliament and Representative Government',paragraphs:['The Constitution placed the unicameral State Great Hural at the center of national lawmaking. Elections would determine its membership, while legislation, government formation, public finance, and oversight linked representation to the exercise of state power.','Parliamentary government did not eliminate political concentration or conflict. It created recurring procedures through which voters, parties, representatives, and cabinets could contest authority and be judged over time.'],sourceIds:['source-mongolia-constitution-1992','source-fish-inner-asian-anomaly-2001']},
    {id:'presidency-government-balance',number:'06',title:'Presidency, Government, and Constitutional Balance',paragraphs:['The new order combined a directly elected president with a government responsible within a parliamentary system. The president, prime minister, cabinet, and State Great Hural received different constitutional roles rather than forming one undivided executive.','This distribution of authority created checks but also recurring questions about appointment, responsibility, and political leadership. Later disputes and amendments would show that constitutional balance is practiced through institutions, not settled permanently by one text.'],sourceIds:['source-mongolia-constitution-1992','source-fish-inner-asian-anomaly-2001']},
    {id:'rights-courts-review',number:'07',title:'Rights, Courts, and Constitutional Review',paragraphs:['The Constitution set out civil, political, social, economic, and cultural rights alongside duties. It also established judicial independence in law and created the Constitutional Court, or Tsets, to review constitutional questions.','Legal guarantees matter because they provide standards against which state action can be challenged. Their existence, however, must be distinguished from equal access, consistent enforcement, and the outcomes of particular cases. Local self-government and state administration likewise became parts of the new constitutional structure.'],sourceIds:['source-mongolia-constitution-1992']},
    {id:'what-1992-changed',number:'08',title:'What 1992 Changed — and What It Did Not Finish',paragraphs:['By February 1992, Mongolia possessed a new constitutional framework for competitive politics, representative institutions, rights, courts, and divided public authority. Political opening had been converted into a durable legal order rather than left as a revolutionary moment alone.','What followed remained contingent. Elections, parties, administrative capacity, economic hardship, accountability, and public trust would test the framework repeatedly. The Constitution made democratic government possible and governable; it did not make democratic quality automatic.'],sourceIds:['source-mongolia-constitution-1992','source-fish-inner-asian-anomaly-2001']},
  ],
  'chapter-shock-transition-markets-privatization': [
    {id:'economic-system-breaks-apart',number:'01',title:'The Economic System Breaks Apart',paragraphs:['Mongolia entered the 1990s as the Soviet-centered economic relationships that had supplied trade, finance, fuel, technical support, and markets were rapidly contracting. This external rupture struck an economy whose production and distribution had been organized through planning rather than market prices.','Political democratization and economic breakdown occurred together, but one did not simply cause the other. The loss of established external support, inherited economic structures, domestic policy decisions, and institutional weakness interacted to produce the transition crisis.'],sourceIds:['source-world-bank-mongolia-market-economy-1991','source-rossabi-modern-mongolia-2005']},
    {id:'planned-to-market-reform',number:'02',title:'From Planned Economy to Market Reform',paragraphs:['Reformers sought to replace administrative allocation with markets, private ownership, commercial banking, and new fiscal and legal institutions. The change required more than permitting trade: prices, enterprise governance, money, taxation, property, and regulation all had to be reorganized.','Mongolian leaders made policy choices under severe financial pressure while receiving external advice and assistance. Reform was therefore neither wholly imposed from abroad nor designed in isolation from international institutions.'],sourceIds:['source-world-bank-mongolia-market-economy-1991','source-world-bank-mongolia-privatization-1992']},
    {id:'prices-economic-shock',number:'03',title:'Price Liberalization and Economic Shock',paragraphs:['As administered prices and supply arrangements changed, households and enterprises faced inflation, shortages, uncertain income, and disrupted production. Price reform could reveal scarcity and enable exchange, but it also transferred risk rapidly to people with very unequal resources.','The severity of hardship cannot be explained by “shock therapy” alone. Policy sequence, monetary instability, collapsing trade, enterprise weakness, social protection, and household location all affected how reform was experienced.'],sourceIds:['source-world-bank-mongolia-market-economy-1991','source-rossabi-modern-mongolia-2005']},
    {id:'voucher-privatization',number:'04',title:'Voucher Privatization',paragraphs:['Voucher privatization attempted to distribute claims on state assets broadly and to transfer enterprises into new forms of ownership. Formal allocation, however, was only the beginning: information, management, secondary exchange, regulation, and the capacity to use or retain assets shaped later outcomes.','It is therefore misleading either to equate vouchers with equal economic benefit or to treat them as the automatic cause of every later inequality. Program design and the institutions surrounding ownership mattered.'],evidenceNote:{claim:'Who benefited from privatization?',sourceBasis:'Contemporary World Bank program research and later historical interpretation',evidenceType:'INSTITUTIONAL RESEARCH / MODERN SCHOLARSHIP',treatment:'ESTABLISHED POLICY / INTERPRETED DISTRIBUTION / DEBATED OUTCOME',confidence:'ESTABLISHED / INTERPRETED',caution:'Formal ownership transfer does not establish equal realized value, and later inequality cannot be assigned to one policy alone.'},sourceIds:['source-world-bank-mongolia-privatization-1992','source-rossabi-modern-mongolia-2005']},
    {id:'livestock-pastoral-economy',number:'05',title:'Privatizing Livestock and the Pastoral Economy',paragraphs:['Collective livestock was transferred into private ownership, making households more directly responsible for herds, production, movement, and risk. For some families animals offered subsistence and an economic base when urban and industrial employment became uncertain.','Pastoral privatization did not produce one outcome. Initial holdings, labor, knowledge, pasture access, markets, weather, and household composition affected whether livestock provided security or exposed families to new vulnerability.'],sourceIds:['source-world-bank-mongolia-privatization-1992','source-rossabi-modern-mongolia-2005']},
    {id:'new-financial-institutions',number:'06',title:'Building New Financial Institutions',paragraphs:['A market economy required institutions for banking, company ownership, exchange, accounting, and public finance. The Mongolian Stock Exchange was established within the privatization process as a mechanism through which enterprise shares could be organized and traded.','Creating an exchange did not by itself produce efficient firms, informed investors, or a mature capital market. Institutional rules, enforcement, management, and trust had to develop alongside formal structures.'],sourceIds:['source-world-bank-mongolia-privatization-1992','source-world-bank-mongolia-market-economy-1991']},
    {id:'households-social-adjustment',number:'07',title:'Households, Poverty, and Social Adjustment',paragraphs:['Transition entered daily life through employment loss, uncertain wages and pensions, changing prices, altered consumption, and pressure on education, health, and other services. Families combined wages, livestock, petty trade, informal work, migration, and mutual support in different ways.','Urban workers, pastoral households, women and men, younger and older people, and families with different assets did not experience one identical transition. Household adaptation demonstrated agency without making hardship voluntary or evenly distributed.'],sourceIds:['source-rossabi-modern-mongolia-2005','source-world-bank-mongolia-market-economy-1991']},
    {id:'international-financial-institutions',number:'08',title:'International Financial Institutions and Reform',paragraphs:['The World Bank, IMF, and Asian Development Bank became important sources of finance, technical advice, and institutional models as Mongolia confronted external shortages and domestic reform. Their involvement influenced policy options and the language through which problems were diagnosed.','External institutions possessed leverage, but Mongolian governments still selected, negotiated, implemented, revised, or struggled with policies. Responsibility for outcomes cannot be assigned entirely either to foreign advisers or to domestic decision-makers.'],sourceIds:['source-world-bank-mongolia-market-economy-1991','source-world-bank-mongolia-privatization-1992']},
    {id:'what-transition-changed',number:'09',title:'What the Transition Changed',paragraphs:['By the end of the decade, private property, markets, new financial institutions, and diversified external relationships had become central to Mongolia’s economy. At the same time, insecurity, unequal capacity, institutional weakness, and changes in social provision shaped the new order.','The transition was neither a simple rescue by capitalism nor economic destruction caused by democracy. It was a compound transformation produced by external rupture, reform choices, adaptation, and institutions whose effects continued well beyond the 1990s.'],sourceIds:['source-world-bank-mongolia-market-economy-1991','source-world-bank-mongolia-privatization-1992','source-rossabi-modern-mongolia-2005']},
  ],
  'chapter-democracy-practice-elections-parties': [
    {id:'democracy-after-constitution',number:'01',title:'Democracy After the Constitution',paragraphs:['After the 1992 Constitution, democratic politics moved from institutional design into repeated practice. Parliamentary and presidential elections, party organization, cabinet formation, courts, and public debate became recurring arenas in which authority was won and contested.','No single election can measure the whole system. Competition, alternation, representation, constitutional restraint, administrative capacity, accountability, and public trust are related but distinct dimensions of democratic development.'],sourceIds:['source-mongolia-constitution-1992','source-gec-election-history','source-fish-inner-asian-anomaly-2001']},
    {id:'presidential-election-1993',number:'02',title:'The 1993 Presidential Election',paragraphs:['In 1993, Mongolian voters participated in the first direct presidential election under the new Constitution. Punsalmaagiin Ochirbat won the office, demonstrating that the presidency would be filled through nationwide electoral competition rather than inherited party appointment.','The election mattered as an institutional milestone, not as a final certificate of democratic success. Presidential authority still operated within relationships linking the Constitution, Parliament, government, courts, parties, and voters.'],sourceIds:['source-gec-election-history','source-mongolia-ochirbat-obituary-2025','source-mongolia-constitution-1992']},
    {id:'parties-new-system',number:'03',title:'Political Parties in a New System',paragraphs:['The former ruling party and new opposition movements entered an electoral system that required organization beyond protest. Parties recruited candidates, built local networks, formed platforms, contested elections, and negotiated governments.','Names alone can conceal change. The Democratic Union movement, electoral coalitions, predecessor parties, and the Democratic Party formed in 2000 belong to connected but not identical organizational histories.'],sourceIds:['source-gec-election-history','source-fish-inner-asian-anomaly-2001']},
    {id:'transfer-power-1996',number:'04',title:'The 1996 Transfer of Parliamentary Power',paragraphs:['The 1996 parliamentary election produced a transfer of governing power to the Democratic Union Coalition. This mattered because an opposition coalition defeated the former ruling party through an election and assumed responsibility for government.','Alternation supplied important evidence of competitive politics, but it did not remove institutional weakness, economic pressure, factional conflict, or accountability problems. A successful transfer was a democratic practice, not a guarantee about every future election.'],evidenceNote:{claim:'Why did the 1996 transfer matter?',sourceBasis:'General Election Commission chronology and comparative democracy scholarship',evidenceType:'OFFICIAL ELECTION RECORD / MODERN SCHOLARSHIP',treatment:'ESTABLISHED ALTERNATION / INTERPRETED SIGNIFICANCE',confidence:'ESTABLISHED / INTERPRETED',caution:'One transfer demonstrates electoral competition without proving permanent democratic consolidation.'},sourceIds:['source-gec-election-history','source-fish-inner-asian-anomaly-2001']},
    {id:'coalitions-turnover',number:'05',title:'Coalition Politics and Government Turnover',paragraphs:['The years after 1996 showed how difficult governing through new parties and coalitions could be. Cabinet turnover, parliamentary bargaining, economic pressure, and disputes over authority tested whether political competition could produce stable and accountable government.','The 2000 parliamentary election again changed the distribution of power. Its result did not mean that one party had dominated permanently from the beginning; it showed that electoral competition could coexist with large parliamentary majorities and uneven opposition capacity.'],sourceIds:['source-gec-election-history','source-fish-deviant-democracy-2008']},
    {id:'party-continuity-change',number:'06',title:'The MPP/MPRP and Democratic Party',paragraphs:['The Mongolian People’s Party had adopted the name Mongolian People’s Revolutionary Party during the socialist period and operated under that name through the transition and early post-socialist decades. It restored the MPP name on 5 November 2010. Continuity of organization did not mean unchanged ideology, leadership, or political practice.','A separately registered MPRP existed from 2011 to 2021 and must not be merged with the canonical MPP/MPRP record. The Democratic Party formed in 2000 from predecessor parties; it likewise should not be projected backward as identical to every earlier democratic movement or coalition.'],sourceIds:['source-mpp-history-2010','source-gec-election-history']},
    {id:'president-parliament-balance',number:'07',title:'President, Parliament, and Political Balance',paragraphs:['Electoral competition operated through more than party totals. A directly elected president, the State Great Hural, the prime minister and cabinet, courts, and constitutional review each occupied different positions in the political system.','When offices were held by competing forces, constitutional relationships could restrain power or generate conflict. When political control was concentrated, formal checks still existed but their practical force depended on law, institutions, and political behavior.'],sourceIds:['source-mongolia-constitution-1992','source-fish-deviant-democracy-2008']},
    {id:'constitutional-electoral-reform',number:'08',title:'Constitutional and Electoral Reform',paragraphs:['Constitutional amendments in 2019 and the separate amendment of 31 May 2023 formed distinct stages in continuing debate over government, Parliament, and representation. The 2023 change expanded the State Great Hural to 126 members, implemented in the 2024 election through a mixed electoral system.','Reform can alter incentives and representation, but enactment alone does not establish better democratic outcomes. The 2024 election must be assessed through legal records, observer findings, participation, implementation, and later institutional practice.'],sourceIds:['source-parliament-constitutional-amendment-2023','source-osce-mongolia-election-2024','source-ipu-mongolia-election-2024']},
    {id:'democracy-ongoing-process',number:'09',title:'Democracy as an Ongoing Process',paragraphs:['Competitive elections, peaceful transfers, party development, civic participation, courts, and constitutional reform support the interpretation that democratic institutions endured. Governance problems, political concentration, barriers to women’s equal political power, and public distrust complicate any triumphal account.','Mongolia’s democracy is therefore best understood as an ongoing institutional practice. Neither constitutional continuity nor political crisis alone answers whether it “succeeded”; the answer depends on which dimension, period, and evidence are being examined.'],sourceIds:['source-fish-inner-asian-anomaly-2001','source-fish-deviant-democracy-2008','source-osce-mongolia-election-2024','source-ipu-mongolia-election-2024']},
  ],
  'chapter-new-foreign-policy': [
    {id:'foreign-policy-after-socialism',number:'01',title:'Foreign Policy After Socialism',paragraphs:['The end of the Soviet-led order transformed Mongolia’s external position. Established economic and security relationships contracted while the country sought new diplomatic, commercial, and institutional connections without changing its location between Russia and China.','Foreign policy after 1990 was therefore neither escape from geography nor passive adjustment. Mongolian governments pursued sovereignty and room for choice within strong structural constraints.'],sourceIds:['source-mongolia-foreign-policy-2011','source-mongolian-third-neighbor-scholarship-2021','source-rossabi-modern-mongolia-2005']},
    {id:'relationship-russia',number:'02',title:'Rebuilding the Relationship with Russia',paragraphs:['Relations with Russia had to be reorganized after the Soviet period. Historical ties, transport networks, trade, energy, diplomacy, and security remained important, but they now operated between formally different political and economic systems.','Russia was neither automatically Mongolia’s protector nor the controller of every modern decision. The relationship combined inherited connections, practical dependence in particular sectors, negotiation, and changing interests on both sides.'],sourceIds:['source-mongolia-foreign-policy-2011','source-mongolia-mfa-third-neighbor','source-rossabi-modern-mongolia-2005']},
    {id:'china-economic-geography',number:'03',title:'China and Economic Geography',paragraphs:['China became central to Mongolia’s trade, especially as mineral exports and cross-border transport expanded. Proximity, border infrastructure, market demand, investment, and political relations made the southern relationship economically consequential.','High trade concentration creates exposure to demand, prices, and border conditions. It does not by itself establish Chinese ownership of Mongolia’s resources or political control over the Mongolian state.'],sourceIds:['source-mongolia-foreign-policy-2011','source-un-mongolia-common-analysis-2023']},
    {id:'greater-diplomatic-space',number:'04',title:'The Search for Greater Diplomatic Space',paragraphs:['Mongolia expanded relations beyond its two neighbors through bilateral partnerships and participation in international organizations. Diversification could provide development cooperation, education, investment, diplomatic support, and security engagement of different kinds.','These relationships did not cancel the importance of Russia and China. They widened Mongolia’s options and networks while leaving geography, trade routes, and unequal economic scale intact.'],sourceIds:['source-mongolia-foreign-policy-2011','source-mongolian-third-neighbor-scholarship-2021']},
    {id:'third-neighbor-idea',number:'05',title:'The Third Neighbor Idea',paragraphs:['Third Neighbor language emerged before it was consolidated into later doctrine. Over time, the term became a way to describe practical diversification toward democratic and economically advanced partners beyond Russia and China.','The 2010 National Security Concept and 2011 Foreign Policy Concept marked stages of formalization rather than a sudden invention in 2011. Term, concept, doctrine, and implementation developed on related but different timelines.'],evidenceNote:{claim:'Does Third Neighbor policy balance Russia and China?',sourceBasis:'Mongolian foreign-policy documents and Mongolian scholarship on the concept’s chronology',evidenceType:'OFFICIAL POLICY / MONGOLIAN SCHOLARSHIP',treatment:'ESTABLISHED DOCTRINE / INTERPRETED EFFECT',confidence:'ESTABLISHED / INTERPRETED',caution:'Diversification creates diplomatic options but does not eliminate geographic or economic asymmetry.'},sourceIds:['source-mongolia-foreign-policy-2011','source-mongolian-third-neighbor-scholarship-2021','source-mongolia-mfa-third-neighbor']},
    {id:'different-third-neighbors',number:'06',title:'Japan, the United States, South Korea, and Other Partners',paragraphs:['Japan, the United States, South Korea, India, and other partners entered Mongolia’s external relationships through different combinations of development cooperation, education, trade, investment, diplomacy, and security engagement. They did not form one uniform bloc.','The value of diversification lay partly in these differences. A development partner, export market, educational destination, and security partner could each widen external connections without playing the same role or carrying equal weight.'],sourceIds:['source-mongolia-foreign-policy-2011','source-mongolia-mfa-third-neighbor','source-mongolian-third-neighbor-scholarship-2021']},
    {id:'multilateral-security-engagement',number:'07',title:'Multilateral Diplomacy, Nuclear Status, and Peacekeeping',paragraphs:['Mongolia used the United Nations and other multilateral institutions to reinforce sovereignty and international participation. Its nuclear-weapon-free status became part of a security strategy based on legal recognition and diplomacy rather than alliance power.','From 2002, participation in UN peacekeeping provided another visible form of engagement. It strengthened international experience and relationships without turning Mongolia into a major global military power. Accession to the World Trade Organization in 1997 similarly connected external strategy to economic institutions.'],sourceIds:['source-un-mongolia-nuclear-free-2000','source-un-mongolia-peacekeeping','source-wto-mongolia-accession']},
    {id:'balancing-geography',number:'08',title:'Balancing Without Escaping Geography',paragraphs:['Mongolia’s post-socialist foreign policy combined necessary relationships with Russia and China, differentiated Third Neighbor partnerships, and multilateral diplomacy. Trade, infrastructure, energy, security, and political values did not always point in the same direction.','This was not perfect balance and did not remove dependence. Its historical significance lies in sustained Mongolian efforts to preserve agency, diversify relationships, and manage asymmetry while remaining anchored in the geography of Inner Asia.'],sourceIds:['source-mongolia-foreign-policy-2011','source-mongolia-mfa-third-neighbor','source-mongolian-third-neighbor-scholarship-2021']},
  ],
  'chapter-mining-growth-natural-resources': [
    {id:'minerals-post-socialist-economy',number:'01',title:'Minerals in the Post-Socialist Economy',paragraphs:['After 1990, Mongolia’s mineral economy expanded within a new system of private investment, state regulation, international finance, and export markets. Copper, coal, and gold connected underground resources to public revenue, employment, infrastructure, and political debate.','Mining did not automatically make the country wealthy or determine how gains were distributed. Project timing, commodity prices, taxation, ownership, public institutions, local effects, and trade routes shaped what extraction meant in different periods.'],sourceIds:['source-world-bank-mongolia-economic-update-2024','source-mongolia-eiti-2020']},
    {id:'erdenet-continuity',number:'02',title:'Erdenet and Continuity Across the Transition',paragraphs:['The Erdenet copper-molybdenum complex continued operating across the change from socialism to a market economy. Its persistence shows that post-1990 mining was built partly through the transformation of inherited industrial capacity, not only through entirely new projects.','Erdenet the city and industrial complex must be distinguished from Erdenet Mining Corporation, the operating company. Changes in corporate and state arrangements belong to the history of the enterprise; they do not make the place and company the same entity.'],sourceIds:['source-mongolia-eiti-2020','source-rossabi-modern-mongolia-2005']},
    {id:'oyu-tolgoi-emerges',number:'03',title:'Oyu Tolgoi Emerges',paragraphs:['Exploration in the South Gobi identified the Oyu Tolgoi copper-gold deposit as a project of exceptional scale. Ivanhoe Mines led its early corporate development, and the 2009 investment agreement established the framework through which the Mongolian state and foreign investors would advance the mine.','Oyu Tolgoi is a deposit and project location; Oyu Tolgoi LLC is the operating company. Rio Tinto, Ivanhoe Mines, Turquoise Hill Resources, and Erdenes Oyu Tolgoi occupied changing corporate or ownership roles and must not be treated as interchangeable names.'],sourceIds:['source-ot-investment-agreement-sec-2009','source-rio-tinto-oyu-tolgoi-2024']},
    {id:'ownership-investment-state',number:'04',title:'Ownership, Investment, and the State',paragraphs:['The 2009 arrangement provided for a 34 percent Mongolian state interest and a 66 percent investor interest in Oyu Tolgoi LLC. Corporate control and the investor-side structure changed as Ivanhoe became Turquoise Hill and Rio Tinto increased its role.','In 2022, Rio Tinto acquired the remaining Turquoise Hill shares, while a separate 2022 reset addressed financing and relations with the Mongolian government. Underground production began in March 2023. This sequence prevents a single ownership snapshot from being projected across the project’s whole history.'],evidenceNote:{claim:'How did Oyu Tolgoi ownership and control change?',sourceBasis:'2009 agreement filing, EITI disclosure, and Rio Tinto corporate chronology',evidenceType:'LEGAL FILING / EXTRACTIVE DISCLOSURE / COMPANY SOURCE',treatment:'ESTABLISHED AGREEMENT AND CORPORATE CHRONOLOGY / INTERESTED SOURCE',confidence:'ESTABLISHED',caution:'Company records support ownership and milestones, not independent conclusions about fairness, benefit, consent, or environmental safety.'},sourceIds:['source-ot-investment-agreement-sec-2009','source-mongolia-eiti-2020','source-rio-tinto-oyu-tolgoi-2024']},
    {id:'tavan-tolgoi-coal',number:'05',title:'Tavan Tolgoi and Coal',paragraphs:['The Tavan Tolgoi deposit became central to Mongolia’s coal-export economy and to debates over state participation, infrastructure, and the use of mineral revenue. Its location near the Chinese market made rail, roads, border capacity, and demand especially consequential.','The deposit and Erdenes Tavan Tolgoi, the state-owned company associated with its development, are distinct entities. Coal’s national importance did not give every project, community, or shareholder the same interests.'],sourceIds:['source-mongolia-eiti-2020','source-world-bank-mongolia-economic-update-2024']},
    {id:'growth-revenue-cycles',number:'06',title:'Growth, Revenue, and Commodity Cycles',paragraphs:['Mineral investment and exports supported growth, government revenue, jobs, and infrastructure. They also increased exposure to construction cycles, global mineral prices, financing conditions, and decisions about taxation, saving, borrowing, and public spending.','Periods of high revenue could expand policy choices while downturns exposed fiscal vulnerability. Whether mining wealth produced durable public benefit depended on institutions and distribution as well as on the value extracted.'],sourceIds:['source-world-bank-mongolia-economic-update-2024','source-mongolia-eiti-2020']},
    {id:'china-export-economy',number:'07',title:'China and the Export Economy',paragraphs:['Dated trade evidence shows that China became the dominant destination for Mongolian mineral exports. Geography, border crossings, transport infrastructure, and Chinese demand concentrated opportunity and risk in the southern trade relationship.','Export concentration is not the same as investment, corporate ownership, or political sovereignty. Economic exposure can constrain choices without demonstrating that China controls Mongolia or owns every major mineral project.'],sourceIds:['source-world-bank-mongolia-economic-update-2024','source-un-mongolia-common-analysis-2023']},
    {id:'communities-water-pasture',number:'08',title:'Communities, Water, Pasture, and Environment',paragraphs:['In mining regions, extraction interacted with water access, pasture, livestock mobility, employment, compensation, local government, infrastructure, and migration. These relationships could create jobs and services while also producing conflict over resources, authority, and environmental change.','Research around Oyu Tolgoi and community agreements documents local negotiation and unequal impacts. A South Gobi case cannot represent every herder, mine, or district, and herders were decision-makers and negotiators as well as people exposed to pressure.'],sourceIds:['source-sneath-mining-pastoralism-2022','source-byambajav-community-agreements-2020']},
    {id:'wealth-dependence-resource-debate',number:'09',title:'Wealth, Dependence, and the Resource Debate',paragraphs:['The resource-curse concept draws attention to commodity dependence, fiscal volatility, governance incentives, exchange-rate pressure, distribution, and environmental cost. These mechanisms are relevant questions for Mongolia, but the label is an analytical framework rather than an established national diagnosis.','Mining created resources and capacities as well as dependence and conflict. Its historical importance lies in how companies, governments, communities, markets, and environments shaped one another—not in a predetermined story of either prosperity or failure.'],sourceIds:['source-world-bank-mongolia-economic-update-2024','source-byambajav-community-agreements-2020','source-sneath-mining-pastoralism-2022']},
  ],
  'chapter-crisis-protest-democratic-resilience': [
    {id:'democracy-under-stress',number:'01',title:'Democracy Under Stress',paragraphs:['Democratic institutions are tested not only by routine elections but by disputes in which political competition, protest, state authority, rights, and public trust collide. Mongolia’s post-1990 record includes continued elections and transfers alongside moments of severe institutional strain.','The events of July 2008 form the central case because they joined an election dispute to street violence, emergency rule, detention, human-rights review, and an unsettled demand for accountability. These evidence layers must remain distinct.'],sourceIds:['source-fish-deviant-democracy-2008','source-ohchr-mongolia-cat-2010']},
    {id:'parliamentary-election-2008',number:'02',title:'The 2008 Parliamentary Election',paragraphs:['Mongolia held a parliamentary election on 29 June 2008. Opposition parties and protesters alleged fraud and irregularities, while official administration, observer assessment, party claims, and later legal evidence offered different kinds of record.','The existence of allegations establishes political dispute, not that comprehensive fraud was legally proven. Responsible reconstruction begins by separating the election result from the protest and the violence that followed.'],sourceIds:['source-osce-mongolia-election-2008','source-gec-election-history']},
    {id:'protest-to-violence',number:'03',title:'From Protest to Violence',paragraphs:['On 1 July, a demonstration in Ulaanbaatar escalated. The MPRP headquarters was attacked and burned, property was damaged, and confrontation developed between participants and police or security forces.','A demonstration, acts of violence, policing decisions, and the conduct of particular individuals are not one undifferentiated event. Sources also reflect different institutional positions, so attribution matters when responsibility is discussed.'],sourceIds:['source-nhrcm-july-2008-review','source-us-state-mongolia-human-rights-2008']},
    {id:'deaths-injuries-detention',number:'04',title:'Deaths, Injuries, and Detention',paragraphs:['Five deaths were reported in connection with the unrest and response. Sources also record civilian injuries, injuries among police and emergency personnel, arrests, detention, and allegations of mistreatment.','These categories cannot be added together or treated as descriptions of the same experience. Totals, causes, and responsibility require source-specific attribution, while the deaths remain a grave and established part of the event’s public history.'],sourceIds:['source-nhrcm-july-2008-review','source-ohchr-mongolia-cat-2010','source-us-state-mongolia-human-rights-2008']},
    {id:'state-emergency',number:'05',title:'The State of Emergency',paragraphs:['Presidential Decree No. 194, issued on 1 July 2008, declared a four-day state of emergency in Ulaanbaatar from midnight. It authorized exceptional measures including restrictions on movement, assembly, and most television broadcasting.','The decree is the primary legal record for the declaration and its stated powers. Its description of events represents the official state framing; it is not an independent judgment about every action or allegation.'],evidenceNote:{claim:'What did the July 2008 emergency decree establish?',sourceBasis:'Presidential Decree No. 194 in Mongolia’s Unified Legal Information System',evidenceType:'MONGOLIAN PRIMARY LAW',treatment:'ESTABLISHED LEGAL ACTION / OFFICIAL FRAMING',confidence:'ESTABLISHED',caution:'The decree establishes emergency authority and duration, not electoral fraud, individual guilt, or final legal responsibility.'},sourceIds:['source-mongolia-emergency-decree-194-2008']},
    {id:'human-rights-accountability',number:'06',title:'Human Rights and Accountability',paragraphs:['The National Human Rights Commission of Mongolia reviewed casualties, detention, complaints, and rights concerns. Amnesty International and the UN Committee against Torture also documented allegations and institutional failures concerning arrest, custody, investigation, and accountability.','A human-rights finding, an institutional review, and a criminal judgment are different forms of evidence. They can establish serious concerns and duties to investigate without supplying a definitive verdict in every individual case.'],sourceIds:['source-nhrcm-july-2008-review','source-ohchr-mongolia-cat-2010','source-amnesty-mongolia-july-2008']},
    {id:'settlement-institutional-continuity',number:'07',title:'Political Settlement and Institutional Continuity',paragraphs:['Political actors eventually returned to parliamentary and governmental processes, and Mongolia’s constitutional institutions continued to operate. Continued competition after crisis matters because democratic order survived a moment in which violence and exceptional authority had placed it under pressure.','Institutional continuity did not resolve every question about policing, detention, responsibility, or trust. No authoritative item-level court record in the present source base supports a definitive prosecution narrative, sentence, acquittal, or pardon.'],sourceIds:['source-fish-deviant-democracy-2008','source-ohchr-mongolia-cat-2010']},
    {id:'protest-civil-society-pressure',number:'08',title:'Protest, Civil Society, and Democratic Pressure',paragraphs:['Protest remained part of Mongolian public life beyond 2008. Citizens, journalists, organizations, and political groups used assembly, reporting, advocacy, and institutional channels to challenge decisions and demand accountability.','Civic pressure can strengthen oversight, but protest is not automatically peaceful or representative, and state response is not automatically lawful because it invokes order. Media freedom, legal safeguards, and credible institutions shape how conflict is processed.'],sourceIds:['source-fish-deviant-democracy-2008','source-osce-mongolia-election-2024']},
    {id:'meaning-democratic-resilience',number:'09',title:'What Democratic Resilience Means',paragraphs:['Resilience can mean that elections continue, power remains contestable, constitutional bodies persist, and public action remains possible after crisis. It does not mean that abuse, weak accountability, political concentration, or distrust are unimportant.','Mongolia’s record therefore resists both a perfect-success narrative and a failed-democracy verdict. The historical question is how institutions respond, learn, and remain open to contestation while rights and accountability continue to be tested.'],sourceIds:['source-fish-inner-asian-anomaly-2001','source-fish-deviant-democracy-2008','source-osce-mongolia-election-2024']},
  ],
  'chapter-changing-society-modern-mongolia': [
    {id:'society-in-motion',number:'01',title:'A Society in Motion',paragraphs:['Since 1990, economic reform, private ownership, migration, education, media, religion, technology, and environmental pressure have reshaped how Mongolians live. These processes joined city and countryside rather than producing two separate histories.','Social transformation was uneven. Household resources, gender, age, location, employment, networks, and exposure to environmental risk influenced which opportunities and pressures people encountered.'],sourceIds:['source-un-mongolia-common-analysis-2023','source-nso-mongolia-yearbook-2021']},
    {id:'ulaanbaatar-internal-migration',number:'02',title:'Ulaanbaatar and Internal Migration',paragraphs:['Ulaanbaatar grew through migration and natural increase as people pursued employment, education, services, family connections, and access to urban land and housing. Pastoral risk and severe winters contributed to some movements, but they did not explain every decision.','Research on ger-area settlement shows migrants and existing residents making varied household choices, including movement within the city. “Rural migrant” was not one permanent identity, and urbanization cannot be reduced to people being passively driven from the countryside.'],sourceIds:['source-ger-areas-migration-2019','source-nso-mongolia-yearbook-2021','source-mongolian-dzud-frequency-2021']},
    {id:'ger-districts-infrastructure',number:'03',title:'Ger Districts and Urban Infrastructure',paragraphs:['Ger districts include established and newly arrived households, gers and houses, different forms of tenure, and wide differences in income and security. Their expansion often outpaced networks for piped heat, water, sanitation, roads, and public transport.','Reliance on household heating and uneven services contributed to severe winter air pollution and daily burdens of obtaining fuel and water. These conditions reflect planning, infrastructure, energy, housing, and household constraints—not a single culture or uniform poverty.'],sourceIds:['source-ger-areas-migration-2019','source-world-bank-ulaanbaatar-air-pollution','source-adb-mongolia-urban-development']},
    {id:'households-work-gender',number:'04',title:'Households, Work, and Gender',paragraphs:['Paid work, informal employment, domestic labor, and care were distributed unequally within households. The 2019 National Statistics Office Time Use Survey documents gendered differences in unpaid domestic and caregiving work, while qualitative research identifies care responsibilities and labor-market norms as constraints on women’s employment.','Formal equality did not guarantee equal lived power, and no statistic describes every woman or household. Rural unpaid family work, urban service pressures, childcare, employment, and migration decisions intersected differently across class and place.'],sourceIds:['source-nso-time-use-survey-2019','source-world-bank-mongolia-gender-labor-2018']},
    {id:'pastoral-life-after-privatization',number:'05',title:'Pastoral Life After Privatization',paragraphs:['Private herd ownership placed animals, income, labor, mobility, and risk more directly within household strategies. Herders responded to markets, pasture access, transport, services, credit, insurance, weather, and family labor with unequal resources.','Pastoralism was neither an unchanged tradition nor a livelihood destined to disappear. Herders adapted, invested, cooperated, moved, diversified, or migrated, while exposure to herd loss and distant markets remained substantial.'],sourceIds:['source-rossabi-modern-mongolia-2005','source-mongolian-dzud-frequency-2021']},
    {id:'dzud-climate-environment',number:'06',title:'Dzud, Climate, and Environmental Pressure',paragraphs:['Dzud emerges from interacting hazards and vulnerabilities: drought, snow or ice, extreme temperature, pasture condition, livestock numbers, preparation, mobility, and household capacity. Severe episodes could destroy herds and influence migration without creating one universal trajectory.','Longer-term warming, land degradation, water pressure, mining, and urban air pollution form related but distinct environmental problems. Climate alone does not explain rural change, and herder behavior alone does not explain environmental stress.'],evidenceNote:{claim:'What drives Mongolia’s environmental pressures?',sourceBasis:'Mongolian agricultural and climate-vulnerability research, local mining scholarship, UN synthesis, and a dated urban air-pollution study',evidenceType:'MONGOLIAN SCHOLARSHIP / ENVIRONMENTAL STUDY / INSTITUTIONAL SYNTHESIS',treatment:'ESTABLISHED PRESSURES / INTERPRETED INTERACTION',confidence:'ESTABLISHED / INTERPRETED',caution:'No single hazard, livelihood, or industry explains all environmental outcomes.'},sourceIds:['source-mongolian-dzud-frequency-2021','source-mongolian-climate-vulnerability-2023','source-sneath-mining-pastoralism-2022','source-world-bank-ulaanbaatar-air-pollution']},
    {id:'religion-after-socialism',number:'07',title:'Religion After Socialism',paragraphs:['Buddhist institutions reopened and expanded after socialist restrictions, with Gandan, restored monasteries, religious education, and lay practice becoming visible again. Shamanic practices, Christian communities, and other forms of belief also contributed to a more plural religious landscape.','Religion did not simply return unchanged from a preserved pre-socialist past. Memory, new institutions, global connections, individual practice, and the legacy of repression shaped revival and reinvention.'],sourceIds:['source-abrahms-kavunenko-religious-revival-2012','source-num-contemporary-religion-2024']},
    {id:'culture-media-expression',number:'08',title:'Culture, Media, and New Forms of Expression',paragraphs:['Writers, filmmakers, artists, musicians, and media producers worked within new markets, freedoms, institutions, and global connections. Cultural production combined continuity, revival, commercialization, experimentation, and arguments over national identity and public memory.','Mongolian hip-hop offers one lens into this change. Urban youth used a globally circulating form for Mongolian language, generational identity, and social commentary; it was influential without representing every young person or replacing older musical traditions.'],sourceIds:['source-tsetsentsolmon-culture-civilization-2014','source-marsh-mongolian-hip-hop-2010','source-socialist-postsocialist-mongolia-culture-2021']},
    {id:'script-memory-identity',number:'09',title:'Script, Memory, and Identity',paragraphs:['Traditional Mongolian script returned to education, public culture, and policy after 1990 while Cyrillic remained central to everyday literacy. The 2015 Language Law established a dual-script official-use provision effective from 1 January 2025, separating enacted policy from the uneven work of teaching and implementation.','Modern images of Chinggis Khan likewise entered state symbolism, monuments, tourism, branding, scholarship, and political language. These uses reveal contemporary memory and identity; they are not evidence about the medieval ruler’s actions.'],sourceIds:['source-mongolia-language-law-2015','source-tsetsentsolmon-culture-civilization-2014','source-socialist-postsocialist-mongolia-culture-2021']},
    {id:'digital-mongolia',number:'10',title:'Digital Mongolia',paragraphs:['Internet and mobile connectivity changed access to information, communication, commerce, culture, and political discussion. Digital media connected urban and rural users while differences in infrastructure, devices, skills, age, income, and disability continued to shape participation.','The 2021 household ICT survey provides a dated measure rather than a timeless description. The E-Mongolia system also consolidated public services online by 2021, expanding convenience while raising continuing questions of access, capability, privacy, and institutional quality.'],sourceIds:['source-nso-ict-household-survey-2021','source-mddic-emongolia-2021']},
  ],
  'chapter-mongolia-twenty-first-century': [
    {id:'three-decades-transformation',number:'01',title:'Mongolia After Three Decades of Transformation',paragraphs:['By the twenty-first century, Mongolia’s constitutional democracy, market economy, external relationships, cities, pastoral livelihoods, and cultural life had all been remade by changes beginning around 1990. None developed along a single path or reached a final state.','Contemporary Mongolia is best approached through interacting structures rather than a list of recent events. Institutions, resources, geography, households, environment, and identity create opportunities while also placing limits on one another.'],sourceIds:['source-un-mongolia-common-analysis-2023','source-rossabi-modern-mongolia-2005']},
    {id:'institutions-political-competition',number:'02',title:'Democratic Institutions and Political Competition',paragraphs:['Repeated parliamentary and presidential elections, transfers of power, party competition, constitutional bodies, civil society, and public protest sustained democratic practice. Governance problems, political concentration, accountability failures, and distrust remained distinct tests of democratic quality.','Continuity does not prove perfection, and crisis does not prove failure. Mongolia’s political history shows institutions repeatedly used, challenged, amended, and judged through both domestic participation and external observation.'],sourceIds:['source-gec-election-history','source-fish-inner-asian-anomaly-2001','source-osce-mongolia-election-2024']},
    {id:'constitutional-reform',number:'03',title:'Constitutional Reform',paragraphs:['The 2019 constitutional amendments changed aspects of institutional balance and government operation. A separate amendment adopted on 31 May 2023 expanded the State Great Hural to 126 members and established the framework for the mixed electoral system used in 2024.','Legal reform can change representation and incentives, but enactment alone cannot establish that governance problems were solved. Effects must be assessed through implementation across later elections and institutions.'],sourceIds:['source-mongolia-constitution-1992','source-parliament-constitutional-amendment-2023']},
    {id:'mining-economic-dependence',number:'04',title:'Mining and Economic Dependence',paragraphs:['Mining became a central source of exports, investment, revenue, jobs, and infrastructure while increasing exposure to commodity prices, project cycles, and external demand. Oyu Tolgoi, Tavan Tolgoi, and Erdenet illustrate different combinations of new investment, state participation, and inherited industry.','Aggregate growth did not determine household benefit, local consent, environmental safety, or good governance. Resource dependence remained a political and institutional question as much as a measure of production.'],sourceIds:['source-world-bank-mongolia-economic-update-2024','source-mongolia-eiti-2020','source-sneath-mining-pastoralism-2022']},
    {id:'ulaanbaatar-uneven-development',number:'05',title:'Ulaanbaatar and Uneven Development',paragraphs:['Ulaanbaatar concentrated population, universities, employment, government, services, finance, and culture. Its expansion also exposed gaps in housing, heat, water, sanitation, transport, planning, and air quality, especially where infrastructure lagged behind settlement.','Urban growth joined opportunity to inequality without dividing the city into a prosperous center and one uniform poor periphery. Ger-district households possessed varied histories, assets, strategies, and claims to urban belonging.'],sourceIds:['source-ger-areas-migration-2019','source-adb-mongolia-urban-development','source-world-bank-ulaanbaatar-air-pollution']},
    {id:'pastoral-environmental-risk',number:'06',title:'Pastoral Society and Environmental Risk',paragraphs:['Pastoral households remained central to Mongolia’s economy and identity while navigating private ownership, markets, services, mining, livestock disease, and climatic risk. Dzud revealed how weather, pasture, herd composition, preparation, mobility, and household resources combine.','Environmental pressure was neither inevitable nor reducible to climate. Policy, infrastructure, local institutions, water use, mining, urban energy, and household adaptation influenced vulnerability and recovery.'],sourceIds:['source-mongolian-dzud-frequency-2021','source-mongolian-climate-vulnerability-2023','source-sneath-mining-pastoralism-2022']},
    {id:'neighbors-third-neighbors',number:'07',title:'Russia, China, and Third Neighbors',paragraphs:['Mongolia continued to manage indispensable relationships with Russia and China while using Third Neighbor partnerships and multilateral institutions to diversify diplomacy, development, education, investment, and security cooperation.','Diversification preserved agency without eliminating geography. China-centered export exposure, Russian-linked infrastructure and energy relationships, and varied outside partnerships remained different forms of connection rather than one simple balance.'],sourceIds:['source-mongolia-foreign-policy-2011','source-mongolia-mfa-third-neighbor','source-mongolian-third-neighbor-scholarship-2021']},
    {id:'culture-identity-digital-change',number:'08',title:'Culture, Identity, and Digital Change',paragraphs:['Religious revival, script policy, literature, film, music, visual culture, public memory, and global exchange transformed cultural life. Continuity and reinvention occurred together rather than along a scale from traditional to modern.','Digital media and online public services added new spaces for expression and administration. Evidence from 2021 documents a particular stage of connectivity and E-Mongolia development, not a permanent level of access or a universal experience.'],sourceIds:['source-tsetsentsolmon-culture-civilization-2014','source-socialist-postsocialist-mongolia-culture-2021','source-nso-ict-household-survey-2021','source-mddic-emongolia-2021']},
    {id:'parliamentary-order-2024',number:'09',title:'The 2024 Parliamentary Order',paragraphs:['The June 2024 election was the first held for the expanded 126-member State Great Hural under the mixed electoral system created by the 2023 amendment. Official and international records document the new structure and its initial implementation.','One election cannot settle the effects of constitutional design on representation, accountability, or political competition. Those consequences require observation across time rather than a permanent judgment made from the first contest.'],sourceIds:['source-parliament-constitutional-amendment-2023','source-osce-mongolia-election-2024','source-ipu-mongolia-election-2024']},
    {id:'achievements-pressures-open-questions',number:'10',title:'Contemporary Mongolia — Achievements, Pressures, and Open Questions',paragraphs:['Mongolia entered the mid-2020s with durable constitutional institutions, competitive politics, a mining-centered economy, active foreign relations, rapid urban and digital change, and living pastoral and cultural traditions. Each achievement remained connected to pressures of inequality, dependence, environment, infrastructure, and trust.','Recent officeholders and disputes are documented current history, not settled verdicts. The era remains open: its significance lies less in choosing between success and failure than in understanding how Mongolians continue to negotiate sovereignty, democracy, livelihood, and identity.'],sourceIds:['source-un-mongolia-common-analysis-2023','source-osce-mongolia-election-2024','source-world-bank-mongolia-economic-update-2024']},
  ],
}

const eraViClosingNarratives = {
  'chapter-pastoral-society-everyday-life-qing': [
    {id:'beyond-qing-court',number:'01',title:'Beyond the Court',paragraphs:['Qing-era Mongol history was lived not only through imperial decisions, noble assemblies, wars, and monasteries, but through households that managed animals, work, movement, property, and institutional demands. Those households were unequal and changed over time.','The evidence is uneven. Archival research is strongest for eighteenth- and nineteenth-century Khalkha, while legal disputes illuminate particular relationships rather than an average household across all Mongol regions.'],sourceIds:['source-munkh-erdene-hereditary-divisions-2016','source-bass-bound-steppe-2021']},
    {id:'households-herds-wealth',number:'02',title:'Households, Herds, and Wealth',paragraphs:['Livestock joined subsistence, property, transport, exchange, status, and institutional obligation. Access to herds and labor therefore shaped household security, while nobles, monasteries, and poorer or dependent households did not command the same resources.','Herding was not an isolated natural economy. Animals and their products connected households with local exchange, religious institutions, caravan work, taxation, and wider markets.'],sourceIds:['source-bass-bound-steppe-2021','source-dear-holy-rollers-2014']},
    {id:'mobility-environment-variation',number:'03',title:'Mobility and Environmental Variation',paragraphs:['Pastoral movement responded to pasture, water, weather, herd composition, household resources, and political boundaries. Banner jurisdictions and monastery domains could shape access as well as administration.','No single route or seasonal timetable describes every household. Evidence from Khalkha cannot automatically describe southern Mongol banners, Dzungar society, Buriat communities, or the entire seventeenth century.'],sourceIds:['source-munkh-erdene-hereditary-divisions-2016','source-bass-bound-steppe-2021']},
    {id:'pastoral-seasons-variable',number:'04',title:'Pastoral Household Through the Seasons',paragraphs:['Spring could bring births, weakened animals, and difficult movement decisions; summer could concentrate pasture use, milk production, and other household work; autumn could involve exchange and winter preparation; winter could intensify shelter, fodder, and mobility constraints.','This is a conceptual guide, not a universal ethnographic law. Patterns varied by region, wealth, herd composition, weather, and political obligation.'],educationalVisual:{title:'Pastoral Household Through the Seasons',variant:'cycle',items:[{title:'Spring',text:'births · herd recovery · movement decisions'},{title:'Summer',text:'pasture · milk · household production'},{title:'Autumn',text:'herd management · exchange · preparation'},{title:'Winter',text:'shelter · constraints · environmental risk'}],note:'Patterns varied by region, wealth, herd composition, weather, and political obligation.'},sourceIds:['source-bass-bound-steppe-2021']},
    {id:'labor-status-obligation',number:'05',title:'Labor, Status, and Obligation',paragraphs:['Documented Khalkha households included people with different legal and social positions. State-mandated service or corvée, pastoral and domestic work, and later market transport labor were related but were not the same institution.','Archival evidence shows changing categories of enslavement and dependency, including substitution for service obligations and incorporation into households. Translating every historical status as either “slave” or “serf” would erase changes in law, kinship, taxation, and lived position.'],sourceIds:['source-bass-bound-steppe-2021','source-dear-holy-rollers-2014']},
    {id:'women-property-marriage',number:'06',title:'Women, Property, Marriage, and Dowry',paragraphs:['In documented Outer Mongolian cases, dowry livestock formed significant property. Courts could recognize women’s claims to dowry animals and their increase, including in divorce disputes. These records make women visible as economic and legal actors.','A litigated case is not a universal rule. Women’s position varied with household status, wealth, region, period, and the circumstances preserved by an archive; evidence about women in bonded households likewise reveals inequality without describing every woman’s experience.'],evidenceNote:{claim:'Women could hold and defend claims to dowry livestock in documented Qing-period Outer Mongolian disputes.',sourceBasis:'Chuluunbaatar and Rogers, read alongside Bass’s Khalkha household research',evidenceType:'ARCHIVAL STUDY / LEGAL CASE / MONGOLIAN DOCUMENT',treatment:'Documented cases with regional, class, and chronological limits',confidence:'REGIONALLY DOCUMENTED',caution:'Court cases prove particular claims and legal practices, not identical rights or outcomes for all women.'},sourceIds:['source-chuluunbaatar-rogers-women-property-2020','source-bass-bound-steppe-2021']},
    {id:'banners-monasteries-daily-life',number:'07',title:'Banners, Nobles, and Monasteries in Daily Life',paragraphs:['Banner and jasagh authority organized jurisdiction and obligations while preserving unequal hereditary positions. Households encountered Qing rule through such layered local institutions, not only through distant imperial officials.','Monasteries were also part of lay society: they received donations, held resources, provided religious and educational functions, and participated in work and exchange. Their relationships with households differed by institution and locality.'],sourceIds:['source-munkh-erdene-hereditary-divisions-2016','source-dear-holy-rollers-2014','source-bass-bound-steppe-2021']},
    {id:'risk-survival-why-life-matters',number:'08',title:'Risk, Survival, and Why Everyday Life Matters',paragraphs:['Weather and herd losses could threaten survival, but vulnerability was mediated by wealth, status, obligations, institutional access, and changing relief practices. Nineteenth-century Khalkha evidence suggests climatic crises could also alter fiscal relationships among commoners, nobles, and clergy.','Mongolian history cannot be understood only through rulers and wars. Household labor, property, gender, dependency, mobility, and environmental risk reveal how imperial institutions were experienced, negotiated, and changed from below.'],sourceIds:['source-bass-bound-steppe-2021','source-chuluunbaatar-rogers-women-property-2020']},
  ],
  'chapter-trade-tea-silver-debt-frontier': [
    {id:'pastoral-commercial-economy',number:'01',title:'A Pastoral Economy Was Not an Isolated Economy',paragraphs:['Pastoral production and commerce were interdependent. Households supplied livestock, animal products, pack animals, knowledge, and labor while obtaining tea, textiles, grain where locally important, silver, and other goods.','The useful distinction is not between “nomadic Mongols” and “commercial outsiders,” but among actors who entered markets with unequal resources, privileges, obligations, and bargaining power.'],sourceIds:['source-dear-holy-rollers-2014','source-wang-irrigation-commercialization-2014']},
    {id:'livestock-goods-circulation',number:'02',title:'Livestock, Tea, Textiles, and Silver',paragraphs:['Livestock could be household wealth, subsistence, transport capacity, tribute or obligation, and a commodity. Tea and textiles moved inward through merchant and caravan networks, while silver connected prices, taxes, credit, and repayment.','These exchanges did not form one universal chain. Routes, commodities, grain production, monetary access, and market conditions differed between Khalkha, Inner Mongolian regions, and border centers and changed across the eighteenth to early twentieth centuries.'],sourceIds:['source-dear-holy-rollers-2014','source-wang-irrigation-commercialization-2014','source-cambridge-sino-russian-relations-1978']},
    {id:'caravan-transport-labor',number:'03',title:'Caravan Transport and Labor',paragraphs:['Long-distance commerce depended on animals and people: caravan leaders, porters, herders, monastery personnel, and local brokers. Late nineteenth-century records show Mongol labor carrying goods between Chinese and Russian commercial worlds.','Transport could be paid market labor, institutional service, or an obligation involving pack animals. Treating all of these as the same form of work would conceal who commanded labor and who bore its risks.'],sourceIds:['source-dear-holy-rollers-2014','source-pozdneyev-mongolia-mongols-1892']},
    {id:'merchant-networks-regulation',number:'04',title:'Merchant Networks and Qing Regulation',paragraphs:['Merchant firms worked through licenses, routes, political relationships, shops, credit, and local institutions. Regulation could restrict commerce while also giving authorized firms structured access to Mongol markets.','Commerce was never explained by merchant ethnicity alone. Mongol nobles, monasteries, households, caravan workers, and officials borrowed, supplied, transported, regulated, and brokered exchange.'],sourceIds:['source-dear-holy-rollers-2014','source-cambridge-sino-russian-relations-1978','source-ewing-qing-policies-mongolia-1980']},
    {id:'credit-debt-pressure',number:'05',title:'Credit, Debt, and Unequal Relationships',paragraphs:['Credit enabled exchange where cash or silver was constrained, but it also created obligations. Evidence from the late Qing documents indebted nobles, monasteries, and some households; repayment and commercial dependence could become serious economic and political pressures.','Those relationships could be unequal, coercive, or exploitative. Yet poverty cannot responsibly be assigned to one ethnic group: borrowers, firms, officials, taxation, transport costs, land pressure, monetary conditions, and political protection formed historically specific systems.'],evidenceNote:{claim:'Did Chinese merchants cause nineteenth-century Mongolian poverty?',sourceBasis:'Dear, Lai, Yi Wang, and Ewing',evidenceType:'MODERN SCHOLARSHIP / ARCHIVAL STUDY / COMMERCIAL RECORD',treatment:'Credit importance established; particular debts documented; wider pressure interpreted; ethnic monocausality rejected',confidence:'ESTABLISHED / DOCUMENTED / INTERPRETED',caution:'Rejecting collective ethnic blame does not sanitize unequal or exploitative credit relationships, and late-period cases cannot describe the entire Qing era.'},sourceIds:['source-dear-holy-rollers-2014','source-lai-kulun-treasury-merchants-2014','source-wang-irrigation-commercialization-2014','source-ewing-qing-policies-mongolia-1980']},
    {id:'nobles-monasteries-economic-actors',number:'06',title:'Nobles and Monasteries as Economic Actors',paragraphs:['Nobles and monasteries were not outside the economy. They controlled resources, received obligations and donations, consumed goods, owned livestock, borrowed, and participated in transport and commercial networks.','Their authority could support exchange while shifting labor and financial burdens onto others. Institutional scale and practice varied, so neither “monasteries only consumed” nor “religious institutions were harmless” is adequate.'],sourceIds:['source-dear-holy-rollers-2014','source-lai-kulun-treasury-merchants-2014']},
    {id:'khuree-institutional-finance',number:'07',title:'Khüree and Institutional Finance',paragraphs:['Archival research on the Jebtsundamba treasury office shows an unusually large religious establishment managing livestock, expenditure, merchant relationships, borrowing, and debt. Khüree developed as a religious, political, and commercial center.','This establishment was not a typical monastery. Its scale makes it important for understanding institutional finance, but its livestock holdings and borrowing cannot be used as an average for all Mongol religious communities.'],sourceIds:['source-lai-kulun-treasury-merchants-2014','source-bareja-starzynska-jebtsundamba-2018','source-dear-holy-rollers-2014']},
    {id:'exchange-network-visual',number:'08',title:'Tea · Livestock · Silver · Credit',paragraphs:['Goods, money, credit, labor, and obligations circulated among several kinds of actor. No single community stood outside this network, and no single relationship explains its inequalities.'],educationalVisual:{title:'Tea · Livestock · Silver · Credit',variant:'network',items:[{title:'Pastoral Households',text:'livestock · products · borrowing'},{title:'Nobles',text:'resources · obligations · credit'},{title:'Monasteries',text:'livestock · donations · finance'},{title:'Merchants',text:'goods · firms · lending'},{title:'Caravan Workers',text:'labor · animals · logistics'},{title:'Qing Structures',text:'licenses · taxes · regulation'},{title:'Border Markets',text:'exchange · silver · changing routes'}],note:'Arrows represent goods, money, credit, labor, and obligations—not fixed ethnic roles.'},sourceIds:['source-dear-holy-rollers-2014','source-lai-kulun-treasury-merchants-2014']},
    {id:'why-frontier-commerce-matters',number:'09',title:'Why Frontier Commerce Matters',paragraphs:['Commerce did not arrive from outside to replace a purely subsistence world. Pastoral society generated goods, transport, labor, demand, and credit relationships that joined local livelihoods to imperial and cross-border economies.','Understanding those connections explains both opportunity and pressure: exchange moved necessary goods and supported institutions, while debt, unequal authority, and changing markets could intensify vulnerability.'],sourceIds:['source-dear-holy-rollers-2014','source-wang-irrigation-commercialization-2014','source-lai-kulun-treasury-merchants-2014']},
  ],
  'chapter-between-qing-russian-empires': [
    {id:'frontier-between-empires',number:'01',title:'A Frontier between Empires',paragraphs:['Qing and Russian imperial power structured the northern Mongol frontier through diplomacy, jurisdiction, commerce, and security. The frontier was not an empty line between two capitals: communities lived, worked, worshipped, and negotiated within it.','Mongol and Buriat actors did not possess equal power with either empire, but inequality did not make them passive. Local knowledge, animals, labor, translation, authority, and religious connections helped make imperial systems function.'],sourceIds:['source-cambridge-sino-russian-relations-1978','source-dear-holy-rollers-2014']},
    {id:'kyakhta-system-1727',number:'02',title:'The Kyakhta System after 1727',paragraphs:['The Treaty of Kyakhta established a diplomatic and border framework within which regulated commerce developed around paired frontier settlements. Officials, rules, and physical places formed related but distinct parts of the system.','Kyakhta did not remain unchanged from 1727 to 1911. Diplomatic interruptions, shifting routes, changing commodities, state policies, and nineteenth-century commercial transformation altered its importance and operation.'],sourceIds:['source-cambridge-sino-russian-relations-1978']},
    {id:'diplomacy-border-regulation',number:'03',title:'Diplomacy and Border Regulation',paragraphs:['Treaties expressed official intentions about borders, communication, and commerce, but rules required interpretation and enforcement. Border soldiers, local authorities, merchants, and travelers encountered the difference between a written framework and everyday practice.','Qing and Russian diplomacy served imperial interests. Neither official archive should be treated as a neutral account of Mongol needs or frontier life.'],sourceIds:['source-cambridge-sino-russian-relations-1978','source-dear-holy-rollers-2014']},
    {id:'trade-frontier-routes',number:'04',title:'Trade across the Frontier',paragraphs:['Tea and other goods moved northward while Russian goods and silver entered changing circuits of exchange. Mongol livestock, pack animals, provisions, and transport connected border markets to routes extending through Khüree and other regional centers.','The legal Kyakhta trade was only one layer of a broader frontier economy. Regulation, local exchange, licensed firms, informal movement, and institutional demand changed over time.'],sourceIds:['source-cambridge-sino-russian-relations-1978','source-dear-holy-rollers-2014']},
    {id:'mongol-buriat-intermediaries',number:'05',title:'Mongol and Buriat Intermediaries',paragraphs:['Collective evidence identifies Mongol and Buriat caravan leaders, porters, traders, border soldiers, religious intermediaries, and local authorities. Their language skills, relationships, and geographic knowledge connected communities that imperial categories kept administratively distinct.','Some records preserve individual participants, but the evidence assembled here is too episodic for reliable biographies. Acknowledging collective agency is preferable to manufacturing representative heroes.'],sourceIds:['source-dear-holy-rollers-2014','source-pozdneyev-mongolia-mongols-1892']},
    {id:'transport-religion-mobility',number:'06',title:'Transport, Religion, and Mobility',paragraphs:['Monasteries and lamas participated in the movement of goods as well as religious practice. Pack animals, porters, hospitality, information, and institutional relationships made religious and commercial routes overlap.','This did not make every monastery a merchant firm or every journey commercial. It shows that categories such as religion, labor, and trade describe connected activities rather than sealed worlds.'],sourceIds:['source-dear-holy-rollers-2014']},
    {id:'pozdneyev-observer',number:'07',title:'A Russian Observer in 1892',paragraphs:['Aleksei Pozdneyev’s expedition record offers detailed observations of routes, settlements, monasteries, commerce, and administration during travel in 1892. It is valuable precisely because its author encountered a late nineteenth-century world in motion.','Pozdneyev was a Russian scholarly observer writing for an imperial-era audience. His itinerary shaped what he saw, his interpretations were not neutral ethnography, and the later English translation adds another layer between observation and present readers.'],evidenceNote:{claim:'Pozdneyev’s account is a near-primary Russian observer source.',sourceBasis:'Russian expedition of 1892; original publication in 1896; English scholarly translation in 1971',evidenceType:'RUSSIAN OBSERVER / TRAVEL ACCOUNT / LATER TRANSLATION',treatment:'Contemporary observation contextualized by authorship, purpose, itinerary, and translation',confidence:'SOURCE ACCOUNT',caution:'One traveler’s descriptions cannot establish universal Mongolian social practice.'},sourceIds:['source-pozdneyev-mongolia-mongols-1892']},
    {id:'kyakhta-system-visual',number:'08',title:'Kyakhta Diplomatic + Commercial System',paragraphs:['Kyakhta linked imperial regulation to the work of frontier communities. Trade, diplomacy, religion, and labor crossed the same system without giving every participant the same authority.'],educationalVisual:{title:'Kyakhta Diplomatic + Commercial System',variant:'network',items:[{title:'Qing Imperial System',text:'diplomacy · regulation'},{title:'Mongol Frontier Communities',text:'authority · animals · knowledge'},{title:'Kyakhta',text:'border institutions · exchange'},{title:'Russian Imperial System',text:'diplomacy · commercial interests'},{title:'Lateral Actors',text:'merchants · porters · Buriat intermediaries · monasteries · nobles · households'}],note:'The relationships changed over time; this is a conceptual system, not a map or a fixed chain of command.'},sourceIds:['source-cambridge-sino-russian-relations-1978','source-dear-holy-rollers-2014']},
    {id:'not-passive-buffer',number:'09',title:'Mongolia Was Not Simply a Buffer',paragraphs:['Calling Mongolia a buffer makes imperial strategy appear to be the whole history. Mongol and Buriat communities participated in commerce, logistics, religion, administration, and diplomacy while making choices within unequal political structures.','Agency does not mean equality of power, and Russian interest was not disinterested protection. Frontier history matters because Mongols helped shape the connections through which competing empires pursued their own aims.'],evidenceNote:{claim:'Were Mongols passive between the Qing and Russian empires?',sourceBasis:'Cambridge diplomatic history, Dear’s archival labor history, and source-critical research on 1911',evidenceType:'MODERN SCHOLARSHIP / ARCHIVAL STUDY',treatment:'Imperial structure and community participation established; degree of agency interpreted',confidence:'ESTABLISHED / INTERPRETED',caution:'Rejecting the passive-buffer model does not erase coercion, constraint, or unequal power.'},sourceIds:['source-cambridge-sino-russian-relations-1978','source-dear-holy-rollers-2014','source-feng-esherick-wei-1911-mongolia-2014']},
  ],
  'chapter-late-qing-crisis-road-1911': [
    {id:'changing-nineteenth-century-order',number:'01',title:'A Changing Nineteenth-Century Order',paragraphs:['By the nineteenth century, Qing–Mongol political institutions continued to organize authority, but commerce, fiscal pressure, monastery and noble finance, frontier connections, and administrative ambitions were changing the conditions in which they operated.','Political separation was not the predetermined result of incorporation in 1691. The road to 1911 must be reconstructed through pressures that accumulated unevenly and a final imperial crisis that transformed what was politically possible.'],sourceIds:['source-ewing-qing-policies-mongolia-1980','source-feng-esherick-wei-1911-mongolia-2014']},
    {id:'commerce-debt-late-qing',number:'02',title:'Commerce and Debt',paragraphs:['Trade and credit connected households and institutions to merchant networks and border markets. Late-Qing evidence documents serious debts among particular nobles, monasteries, and households, including financial pressure at the unusually large Jebtsundamba establishment.','Economic pressure mattered, but it was neither uniform nor a complete cause of political rupture. Borrowing, taxation, monetary conditions, transport, official policy, land pressure, and unequal commercial relationships interacted differently by locality.'],sourceIds:['source-dear-holy-rollers-2014','source-lai-kulun-treasury-merchants-2014','source-ewing-qing-policies-mongolia-1980']},
    {id:'nobles-monasteries-politics',number:'03',title:'Nobles, Monasteries, and Political Authority',paragraphs:['Mongol nobles and Buddhist institutions remained political actors, not remnants waiting to disappear. Their authority rested on different combinations of hereditary office, banner jurisdiction, religious position, resources, and relationships with Qing officials.','Changing policy therefore affected institutions capable of negotiation and opposition. Elite politics mattered without representing every pastoral household or producing one unified Mongol response.'],sourceIds:['source-munkh-erdene-hereditary-divisions-2016','source-bareja-starzynska-jebtsundamba-2018','source-elverskog-our-great-qing-2006']},
    {id:'regional-differences-late-qing',number:'04',title:'Different Regions, Different Pressures',paragraphs:['Developments in southern and Inner Mongolian regions cannot automatically describe Khalkha. Cultivation, settlement, banner–provincial relations, commercial penetration, and reform unfolded through distinct local histories.','“Inner Mongolia” and “Outer Mongolia” are useful later administrative and historiographical labels when used carefully; they should not be projected backward as fixed modern national territories or uniform societies.'],sourceIds:['source-wang-irrigation-commercialization-2014','source-wang-land-reform-mongolia-2014','source-ewing-qing-policies-mongolia-1980']},
    {id:'russian-interests-possibilities',number:'05',title:'Russian Interests and Political Possibilities',paragraphs:['Russian commercial, diplomatic, and strategic interests affected the choices available to Mongol leaders. Kyakhta and wider frontier networks created channels of information and contact as well as dependency and competition.','Russia did not simply protect Mongolia from China. Its officials pursued imperial interests, while Mongol actors assessed those interests within their own political projects and constraints.'],sourceIds:['source-cambridge-sino-russian-relations-1978','source-feng-esherick-wei-1911-mongolia-2014']},
    {id:'late-qing-state-building',number:'06',title:'Late-Qing State-Building',paragraphs:['Late-Qing officials pursued stronger administration, revenue, security, and military capacity across an empire under severe pressure. Frontier reform formed part of this broader state-building effort rather than a policy isolated from events elsewhere in the Qing world.','Implementation depended on agencies, provincial authorities, banner institutions, local resources, and political negotiation. Announced reform and lived change were not identical.'],sourceIds:['source-ewing-qing-policies-mongolia-1980','source-wang-land-reform-mongolia-2014']},
    {id:'new-policies-regional',number:'07',title:'The New Policies',paragraphs:['The New Policies combined administrative, fiscal, military, settlement, and—where regionally applicable—agricultural and land measures. In some Mongol regions they increased anxiety about jurisdiction, resources, and the future of established authority.','They were not one uniform program experienced identically everywhere. Inner Mongolian land-reform evidence and Outer Mongolian political evidence illuminate different processes and must not be merged into a single colonization narrative or sole explanation for independence.'],sourceIds:['source-ewing-qing-policies-mongolia-1980','source-wang-land-reform-mongolia-2014']},
    {id:'eighth-jebtsundamba-before-1911',number:'08',title:'The Eighth Jebtsundamba before 1911',paragraphs:['At Ikh Khüree, the Eighth Jebtsundamba held the leading Khalkha Buddhist office. Religious authority and relationships with Mongol nobles gave the institution political significance as the late-Qing crisis developed.','This chapter stops before his revolutionary career. His later title as Bogd Khan, the declaration of independence, and the government formed after the rupture belong to Era VII; reincarnation succession is not biological genealogy.'],sourceIds:['source-bareja-starzynska-jebtsundamba-2018','source-ewing-qing-policies-mongolia-1980','source-feng-esherick-wei-1911-mongolia-2014']},
    {id:'pressures-toward-1911',number:'09',title:'Pressures Leading Toward 1911',paragraphs:['No strand alone produced separation. Long-term institutions, economic pressure, regional difference, noble politics, monastic authority, reform, Russian interest, and the Qing imperial crisis converged without making the outcome inevitable.'],educationalVisual:{title:'Pressures Leading Toward 1911',variant:'convergence',items:[{title:'Economic Pressures'},{title:'Noble Politics'},{title:'Monastic Authority'},{title:'Late-Qing Reform'},{title:'Regional Difference'},{title:'Russian Interest'},{title:'Qing Imperial Crisis'},{title:'Political Rupture Becomes Possible'}],note:'The strands converge contingently; this is not a line from 1691 to inevitable independence.'},evidenceNote:{claim:'Why did political separation become possible in 1911?',sourceBasis:'Ewing, Liping Wang, Feng, Esherick and Wei, and the institutional and economic studies used across Era VI',evidenceType:'MODERN SCHOLARSHIP / ARCHIVAL STUDY / INTERPRETATION',treatment:'Multi-causal model with an established imperial rupture and interpreted contributing pressures',confidence:'ESTABLISHED / INTERPRETED',caution:'No single reform, debt relationship, institution, foreign power, or long-term grievance made independence inevitable.'},sourceIds:['source-ewing-qing-policies-mongolia-1980','source-wang-land-reform-mongolia-2014','source-feng-esherick-wei-1911-mongolia-2014']},
    {id:'threshold-of-new-era',number:'10',title:'At the Threshold of a New Era',paragraphs:['The Qing imperial crisis of 1911 turned accumulated but uneven pressures into an immediate political opening. Mongol nobles and religious leaders acted within that opening; their decisions cannot be reduced to an automatic reaction or a foreign design.','What followed—the declaration of independence, the Bogd Khanate, foreign intervention, revolution, and the creation of new political orders—belongs to Era VII. Era VI ends at the moment rupture became possible, not with its later outcomes already settled.'],sourceIds:['source-feng-esherick-wei-1911-mongolia-2014','source-ewing-qing-policies-mongolia-1980']},
  ],
}

